## Text a C library keeps
#
# `s.cstr()` lends a string to C for one call, and the bytes may be gone by the
# next line. Some libraries keep the pointer and read it later — SQLite when it
# is told not to copy, libcurl for the body of a request — and the header cannot
# say which: a parameter that copies and one that keeps are both `const char *`.
#
# A lease is for that case. `x: cstr @ s.lease()` hands C a COPY of the bytes
# that lives until `end_lease(@x)` frees it and empties the cell. The name of a
# lease may stand only as an argument of a call, nothing else writes its cell,
# and a lease nobody ends is named when the program exits, so a leak is a loud
# exit and never a silent one. The C side here is the smallest library that
# keeps what it is given: `13-lease.h`, beside this file.

extern "13-lease.h"
    function keep_label(s: cstr)
    function kept_label_length() -> u64

function main()
    at: i64 @ 0

    while at < 3
        label: cstr @ f"row-{at}-payload".lease()
        keep_label(s: label)
        print(f"C still reads {kept_label_length()} bytes")
        end_lease(@label)
        at @ at + 1
